Silicon India reports Jai Shankar also met President John Dharmani Mahama on the first

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.240
stop of a three-country African tour.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:56.600
In Zimbabwe, wreckage from the helicopter craft that killed businessman Wiknow Chavayo,

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:01.520
his wife Lucy Muteke, and three others has been moved for forensic examination.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.420
News in Bobbois says the government expects a preliminary report within 30 days.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:11.660
The Jerusalem Post says defense officials are concerned Iran may strike Israel amid

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:12.560
Hormuz tensions.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:18.280
Senior IDF officials told ministers the measures are weighing heavily on Iran's economy,

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:20.860
and that the situation gets worse every day.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:26.060
In the wider world, Iran's envoy to the organization for the prohibition of chemical

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:33.040
weapons, Hadi Faragevand, told Middle East I that U.S. strikes have damaged 111 petrochemical

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:38.000
and pharmaceutical plants across Iran. He said those hits have disrupted supply chains

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.820
and work under the chemical weapons convention. U.S. Vice President JD Vance said Tehran

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:48.880
must cut its nuclear enrichment capacity in a meaningful way if it wants to meet Washington

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.440
and end the war. He said the United States is still open to a duel, but only with concrete

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:58.600
nuclear concessions, and that talk will not be accepted in place of action.

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:04.040
On food labels in South Africa, the case on both sides is whether a reworked warning

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.640
draft stays shut until the minister publishes it.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:11.440
Daily Maverick reports the Health Department has finished its review of labels for sugary,

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:16.920
salty, and fatty foods, and the new text is now inside Nedlack, more than three years

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:18.380
after the draft first appeared.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:20.880
Nobody outside government has seen it.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:27.300
Spokesperson Foster Mojale says the department took substantive submissions from 179 companies

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:28.240
and organizations.

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:34.540
Captured, that file ran past 6,000 pages, and a 4-person technical team worked through

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:34.740
it.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.160
He says the rewrite was partly meant to spare industry costs it does not need to bear.

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:44.080
Mojale's sequence from here is a talk with the department's own lawyers, then a feedback

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:50.180
session with industry, academia, civil society and consumer advocates, then legal processing,

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.980
then submission to the health minister for publication.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:06:00.240
Legal researcher Dr. Isosa Omaree of PricelessSA says her organization has had no word on

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.520
how those submissions were weighed or folded in.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:07.560
She says the submissions themselves are not public, so Priceless cannot judge whether

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.420
the points people raised were dealt with.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:15.480
Daily Maverick notes that under the draft regulations, 80% of packaged products in South

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:17.300
Africa would need warning labels.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:22.640
What both accounts share is this, a formal public consultation already happened.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:28.080
The new text has not been shown outside government, and it is being argued behind closed doors

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:28.840
at Nedlack.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:35.180
The next step Mojale names is that legal discussion, then the feedback session, then the file

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:36.120
goes to the minister.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:41.500
A sign of progress is on a factory floor in Ogun State, where harvest field health care

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:46.720
has begun commercial production of Sinera duo forte, a next-generation treated mosquito

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:51.660
real news magazine says operations at the free trade zone plant started on the

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:56.100
13th of September and the site is already feeding a World Bank funded program

00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:01.380
that reaches 31 states. It is the first plant of this kind in Nigeria that is

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:05.760
wholly Nigerian owned. The nets are made under an agreement with GDM health

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:10.980
products and para pyrethroid with chloropenapyr, a second insecticide that acts

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:14.480
differently for places where mosquitoes resist pyrethroid alone.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:21.020
Scenera Duo Forte is pre-qualified by the World Health Organization, and the Ogun site is an approved

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:27.880
factory under that pre-qualification. The plant is also certified by N-A-F-D-A-C and to ISO

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:34.060
standards. The WHO recommends this class of net where pyrethroid resistance is already established.

